WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:04.400
"Eternals" is expanding the Marvel Cinematic&nbsp;
Universe like few movies have before it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:09.280
with a huge cast of characters, a grand scope,&nbsp;
and backstory that spans thousands of years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:13.120
It's also full of Easter eggs and references&nbsp;
to other movies in the MCU and beyond.

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:16.080
In playing Sersi, Gemma Chan joins the once rare,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:19.120
now-growing “Two-Timers Club” of&nbsp;
the Marvel Cinematic Universe.

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:23.040
In case you don’t recall, Chan played the&nbsp;
Kree sniper Minn-Erva in 2019’s “Captain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:25.520
Marvel,” although you could be&nbsp;
forgiven for not realizing it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:29.920
with the “Crazy Rich Asians” actor being basically&nbsp;
unrecognizable under layers of blue makeup.

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:32.880
She's one of the few actors who played&nbsp;
different characters within the Marvel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:37.360
Cinematic Universe. But when you include other&nbsp;
movies based on Marvel Comics, from Fox, Sony,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:42.080
or Marvel Studios' non-MCU Marvel Knights brand,&nbsp;
many actors have suited up for double duty.

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:46.160
That includes Chris Evans, who played Johnny&nbsp;
Storm in Fox's "Fantastic Four" movies before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:50.480
suiting up as Steve Rogers; Josh Brolin,&nbsp;
who played Cable in Fox's “Deadpool 2" in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:54.560
addition to the MCU's Thanos; Idris Elba,&nbsp;
who appeared as in Moreau in “Ghost Rider:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:57.920
Spirit of Vengeance” the same year he began&nbsp;
playing Heimdall in the “Thor” movies;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:02.080
Michael B. Jordan, who also played Johnny Storm&nbsp;
before he became Killmonger, and even more.

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:05.680
This group will soon be joined by Aaron&nbsp;
Taylor-Johnson, slated to play "Kraven the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:10.640
Hunter" for Sony, previously seen as Quicksilver&nbsp;
in “Avengers: Age of Ultron,” and Mahershala Ali,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:13.760
soon-to-star in “Blade,” after&nbsp;
previously being seen in “Luke Cage."

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:17.200
"Eternals” is said to take place&nbsp;
roughly eight months after “Avengers:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:20.480
Endgame,” around the same time as the&nbsp;
events of “Spider-Man: Far From Home.”

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:24.000
When Ajak meets with Ikaris, she&nbsp;
mentions the seismic events of “Avengers:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:27.840
Infinity War” and "Endgame." According to&nbsp;
her, the disruptive erasure and return of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:31.200
so many people in the universe provided&nbsp;
the energy necessary to kickstart the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:34.640
emergence -- the world-destroying threat&nbsp;
at the center of "Eternals'" plot.

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:41.760
“Thanos erased half of the population of&nbsp;
the universe. But the people of this planet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:44.480
brought everyone back with the snap of a finger."

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:47.760
Later, Dane Whitman brings up&nbsp;
a big relevant question about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:50.480
the Eternals' involvement -- or&nbsp;
lack thereof -- in those events.

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:54.000
“Why didn’t you guys help fight Thanos? Or any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:56.000
war, or all the other terrible&nbsp;
things throughout history?”

00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:00.960
Sersi justifies the Eternals' absence by saying&nbsp;
that humanity would have never had a chance to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:05.200
develop if the super-beings had spent thousands&nbsp;
of years intervening in every crisis -- which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:09.920
makes sense, even though it's not a good look&nbsp;
-- even Pepper Potts suited up to fight Thanos.

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:12.240
Among other references to&nbsp;
recent events in the MCU,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:15.120
there's a news broadcast about&nbsp;
the movie's suspicious earthquake,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:18.480
and someone can be heard speculating that it&nbsp;
might have something to do with “The Blip.”

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:21.600
Though it's happened plenty&nbsp;
of times in comics, so far,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:24.800
the Marvel and DC universes&nbsp;
have yet to collide on screen.

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:28.640
That lends some significance to a couple&nbsp;
of conspicuous lines in “Eternals.”

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:32.560
The first comes when most of the Eternals get&nbsp;
together for a feast in modern-day Australia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:36.400
with some of the group catching up for the&nbsp;
first time in centuries. When Gilgamesh meets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:40.800
Kingo's documentarian friend, he asks if he&nbsp;
serves the same function as Alfred in Batman.

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:43.600
"Don't wait up, Al!"

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:45.680
"I'll cancel the pizza!"

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:50.000
The second instance comes later in the&nbsp;
film, as Ikaris visits Chicago and the human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:54.400
family of Phastos. When Ikaris meets the son of&nbsp;
Phastos, the boy remarks that he has seen him in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:59.840
TV reports about the Deviants attacks, and that&nbsp;
he recognizes him as a very specific superhero.

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:03.840
"Dad! That's Superman! With the cape! And you&nbsp;
were shooting laser beams out of your eyes!"

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:05.600
"I don't wear a cape."

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:10.720
Now more than ever, it seems safe to say that&nbsp;
most characters in the MCU know who Superman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:13.520
Batman, Wonder Woman, Green&nbsp;
Lantern and the rest are — if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:17.280
only because they’ve seen many of the&nbsp;
same movies and TV shows that we have.

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:18.320
"What's your superpower?"

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:24.080
The MCU has been going strong since 2008,&nbsp;
but Marvel Studios is still trying to rile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:27.920
up today's teens with timely references&nbsp;
as the years go on. Fortnite, anyone?

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:32.560
"Thor, he's back. That kid on the&nbsp;
TV just called me a dickhead again."

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:36.800
The most obvious example of this in&nbsp;
"Eternals" is the movie's post-credits scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:40.880
which brings Harry Styles to the MCU.&nbsp;
But another mega-popular musical act&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:45.120
also gets a shout out in this movie --&nbsp;
the worldwide phenomenon known as BTS.

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:48.560
When we first catch up with modern-day&nbsp;
Kingo, he seems to be having a blast.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:51.840
The Eternal is a Bollywood star who&nbsp;
has convinced the world that his father&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:55.600
(secretly him) and grandfather (also&nbsp;
secretly him) were Bollywood actors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.600 --> 00:03:59.120
from generations past. Because he never&nbsp;
looks any older, Kingo has been starring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:03.200
in Bollywood movies for decades with names&nbsp;
like “Shadow Warrior 2: Voyage of Time.”

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:07.040
As he explains his career in show business to&nbsp;
his so-called “friends from college,” Kingo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:12.160
makes reference to BTS. Within moments, we cut to&nbsp;
the next scene — scored by the BTS song “Friends.”

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:16.039
"Eternals assemble."

00:04:16.039 --> 00:04:17.200
As “Eternals” builds to its conclusion,

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:21.360
the reunited group pulls out all the stops in&nbsp;
an attempt to stop the emergence of Tiamatk.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:25.040
Phastos speculates that if all the Eternals&nbsp;
were to combine their powers, it could be enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:29.280
to create something he dubs “the Uni-mind,”&nbsp;
allowing them to stop the end of the world.

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:33.360
If you’re familiar with the Eternals in the&nbsp;
comics, you know this is no mere speculation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:37.440
The Eternals have indeed employed the Uni-Mind&nbsp;
on multiple occasions, which has sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:41.840
manifested itself on the comic book page as a&nbsp;
gender-less being of light, mind and pure energy.

00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:46.800
Typically, only the Prime Eternal can summon the&nbsp;
Uni-Mind, which is why in the film you see Gemma&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:50.720
Chan’s Sersi taking the lead. The Uni-Mind&nbsp;
never seems to take the form of a being,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:55.360
yet it is clear that remaining Eternals have come&nbsp;
together to support the hypothesis of Phastos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:59.440
linking their minds and becoming more powerful&nbsp;
as a team than they ever could be apart.

